.TH TEXRS 1 "2026-08-30" "texrs 0.4.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
texrs \- a TeX engine: Knuth's mouth and expander, lowered onto a bytecode VM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B texrs
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR[.tex]]... [\fICOMMANDS\fR]
.br
.B texrs
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\e\fIFIRST-LINE\fR
.br
.B texrs
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB&\fIFMT\fR \fIARGS\fR
.br
.B texrs
.SH DESCRIPTION
Invoked the way
.BR tex (1)
is invoked.  A file name without an extension gets
.BR .tex ;
arguments after the file are more TeX input, read after it, unless the file's
own
.B \eend
stopped the run first; a first argument beginning with
.B \e
makes the whole argument list a line of input with no file at all; and
.B texrs
with no arguments opens the prompt, because an engine given nothing to do should
ask rather than print its usage and quit.
.PP
.B texrs
reads a TeX file, tokenises it under a mutable category\-code table, expands
the macros, and lowers what is left to
.B fusevm
bytecode which the shared VM executes.  Nothing in texrs interprets TeX: the
program runs as bytecode, the same way
.BR zshrs ,
.BR stryke ,
.BR rubylang ,
and
.B pythonrs
run on that engine.
.PP
TeX is two machines.  The
.I mouth
turns bytes into tokens; the
.I expander
turns tokens into other tokens \(em
.BR \edef ,
.BR \ecsname ,
.BR \ethe ,
the conditionals.  Only after that does the
.I stomach
build boxes and ship DVI.  texrs implements the first two, which is the half a
macro\-heavy document spends its time in, and a deliberately small third:
.B \-\-pdf
Typeset to
.I FILE.pdf
beside the source, honouring the typeface the document asked for. A DVI can
only name a
.I .tfm
font, so the \fB\-\-dvi\fR path sets everything in Computer Modern however
loudly a document says \fB\\setmainfont\fR; a PDF can name the fourteen faces
every reader has, so the request is honoured here. The mapping is by what the
face IS rather than by its name -- Arimo carries Arial's metrics and Arial
carries Helvetica's, so all three set at the same widths -- and a monospace
request goes to Courier whatever it was called.
.IP
Colour is honoured too: \fB\\textcolor\fR becomes PDF's own colour operator
around the words it covers.
.IP
The fourteen faces are named rather than embedded, so the file is small and
opens anywhere, and the document is NOT set in the font file it names. Line
breaking still measures in Computer Modern's widths, so a line may run a little
long or short of the measure.
.TP
.B \-\-dvi
measures the text in a real
.IR .tfm ,
breaks it into lines with the first break that fits, and ships a page a driver
will open.  It is
.B not
.IR tex.web 's
stomach \(em no hyphenation, no glue stretching or shrinking, no page breaking
by penalties, no maths, no boxes a document can nest, and lines chosen first\-fit
where TeX minimises badness across a whole paragraph.  Without
.B \-\-dvi
.B \eend
stops the run rather than shipping a page.
.PP
What is carried: category codes and
.BR \ecatcode ,
the three\-state line scanner,
.B ^^X
notation,
.B \edef
with undelimited and delimited parameters,
.BR \elet ,
.BR \eedef / \exdef ,
.BR \egdef ,
.BR \eglobal ,
.BR \ebegingroup / \eendgroup
and brace groups,
.BR \ecsname / \eendcsname ,
.BR \estring ,
.BR \ethe ,
.BR \enumber ,
.BR \eexpandafter ,
.BR \enoexpand ,
the conditionals
.RB ( \eiftrue ,
.BR \eiffalse ,
.BR \eifnum ,
.BR \eifodd ,
.BR \eifx ,
.BR \eifcase
with
.BR \eor ,
.BR \eelse ,
.BR \efi ),
the 256
.B \ecount
registers with
.BR \eadvance / \emultiply / \edivide ,
and
.BR \emessage .
For what is not carried, see
.B BUGS.md
in the source tree.
.SH DOCUMENTS
A document larger than one file is described by a
.I Texrs.toml
at its root, and the
.B \-X
commands act on that document rather than on a file \(em the shape
.BR tectonic (1)
gives its own V2 interface.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[doc]
name = "thesis"
inputs = ["macros.tex", "body.tex"]

[[output]]
name = "default"
type = "messages"
.fi
.RE
.PP
A
.B bundle
key names a zip of support files, searched after the document's own directory
and its extra paths: what a document carries with it when it is shared as one
file rather than as a directory somebody has to keep in order.  A file beside
the document overrides the one in its bundle, so a recipient can change a macro
without unpacking the archive.  Unlike
.BR tectonic (1),
whose bundle is fetched, this one is a path \(em a build that reaches the
network is one that fails on an aeroplane and depends on a server still
answering in ten years.  Fetch it with whatever fetches things and hand the file
over.
.PP
A bundle may also be named by the digest of its bytes,
.BR sha256: \fIDIGEST\fR ,
which resolves to one that
.B \-X bundle fetch
has put in the cache.  A URL in that key is refused with the command that
fetches it: a build never reaches the network, so what a document names has to
be bytes that are already here.  Naming the digest rather than the address is
also what makes a build reproducible \(em an address serves whatever it serves
today, a digest is the file the document was written against.
.TP
.B \-X bundle fetch " \fIURL\fR"
Download an archive over http or https and file it under the digest of its
bytes, printing the
.B sha256:
name to put in
.IR Texrs.toml .
This is the only thing in texrs that uses the network, and it does so only when
asked.  A response larger than a hundred megabytes is refused rather than
written: a bundle is macro files, and anything that size is a mistake or a
hostile server.
.TP
.BI \-X " dvi \fIFILE\fR.dvi"
Read what real
.BR tex (1)
shipped for a document and print what is in it: the preamble's comment, each
page with its
.BR \ecount0 ,
the fonts it defines, its specials, and the characters it sets.
.PP
texrs has no stomach yet \(em it stops where the boxes would begin \(em so
nothing in it produces a page today, and this reads what another engine wrote.
The library writes the format as well as reads it, which is what the stomach
will call when there is one; a DVI file is a linked list read backwards, and
its pointers can only be filled in while writing, so
.BR dvitype (1)
is held against what texrs writes.  The parity contract stops in the same
place: today it compares the
.B \emessage
stream, and the moment texrs sets a character the reference to compare against
is what tex shipped. This is the reading half of that comparison, written while
the format can still be checked against a document whose output is known.
XeTeX's extensions (opcodes 252\(en254) are named rather than decoded: pointing
this at an
.I .xdv
should say what the file is rather than fail as if it were damaged.
.TP
.BI \-X " dvi \fIA\fR.dvi \fIB\fR.dvi"
Say whether two files are the same DOCUMENT, and exit non-zero when they are
not.  Not a byte comparison: two engines that typeset one document identically
still write different files \(em the preamble's comment carries the time of the
run, and which movement opcode encodes a displacement is a choice \(em so what
is compared is what a reader would see.  The pages, the characters set, the
rules, the specials and the fonts.  Positions are left out for the same reason:
two correct engines may round a displacement differently.
.PP
The character codes are the font's rather than Unicode, since that is what DVI
carries: in cmr10, code 11 is the
.B ff
ligature.  For comparing two engines it does not matter, because both write the
same code.
.TP
.BI \-X " bib \fIFILE\fR.bib"
Read a bibliography database and print what is in it: the
.B @string
abbreviations it defines, then each entry with its type, key and fields.  Exits
non-zero when something could not be read, so a harness need not grep for the
word warning.
.PP
BibTeX is the other engine in TeX's world and the only one needing neither boxes
nor fonts.  This is its reading half: the database format, with the corners that
make it more than a split on commas \(em a braced value nests, a quoted one is
protected by braces inside it, an abbreviation is expanded and may be
concatenated with
.BR # ,
types and field names are case-insensitive while keys are not, and a key defined
twice keeps the first as bibtex does.  A record that cannot be read is reported
and the rest of the file still reads, because a database should not be lost to
one typo.
.TP
.BI \-X " bib \fIFILE\fR.aux"
Say what a document cites.  The
.I .aux
is what TeX writes while typesetting \(em every
.B \ecite
as a
.BR \ecitation ,
the databases as
.BR \ebibdata ,
the style as
.B \ebibstyle
\(em so reading it answers "which entries does this document actually use"
without running a
.IR .bst .
Each cited entry is printed with its title, each key no database defines is
printed as
.BR MISSING ,
and each entry nobody cited as
.BR uncited .
Exits non-zero when anything is missing, because a citation nothing defines
becomes a
.B ?
in the typeset bibliography.
.PP
The
.B \e@input
files LaTeX writes for each
.B \einclude
are read with it: a reader that stopped at the top would miss every chapter of a
book, and one that is not there yet is not an error.
.TP
.BI \-X " bst \fIFILE\fR.bst"
Read a bibliography style and print what it declares: the fields an entry may
carry, the
.B MACRO
abbreviations a database's month names mean, how many functions it defines, and
the commands that say what it does \(em
.BR READ ,
.BR SORT ,
.BR ITERATE .
.PP
A
.I .bst
is a program in a stack language, and its one common failure is a name nothing
defines.  bibtex reports that at run time, one name per run, in the middle of a
build; this reads the whole style and prints every such name as
.B UNDEFINED
in one pass, exiting non-zero when there is one.  A name counts as defined when
it is a builtin, a
.BR FUNCTION ,
a
.BR MACRO ,
an
.B ENTRY
field or string, or an
.B INTEGERS
or
.B STRINGS
variable \(em and a function may be called before the line that defines it, as
the standard styles do.
.PP
The interpreter is not this: it needs
.BR width$ ,
which measures a string in a font and so needs a
.IR .tfm .
An interpreter that was right for the styles avoiding that and quietly wrong for
the rest would be worse than none.
.TP
.BI \-X " bibtex \fIFILE\fR.aux"
Run the style: read the
.I .aux
for what the document cites, which style it wants and which databases to read;
read those databases with the style's own
.B MACRO
abbreviations defined, which is where
.B month = jan
gets its meaning; run the style; write the
.I .bbl
the document reads back in.  This is what
.BR bibtex (1)
does, and it writes the same file \(em the four styles TeX Live ships are
compared against the real program, whole
.I .bbl
against whole
.IR .bbl ,
in the test suite.
.PP
A
.I .bst
is a program in a stack language, and running one means the 37 builtins:
.BR format.name$ ,
which splits a name into First, von, Last and Jr and renders it through a
format like
.BR "{vv~}{ll}{, jj}{, f.}" ,
ties and all;
.BR change.case$ ,
.B purify$
and
.BR text.prefix$ ,
which treat a brace group beginning with a backslash as one character;
.BR width$ ,
which measures in cmr10; and the sorting and cross-referencing BibTeX does
around them \(em an entry inherits the fields of the entry it cross-references,
and that entry joins the bibliography only if it was cited on its own or is
pointed at twice.
.TP
.BI \-X " itar \fIFILE\fR.tar [" \fINAME\fR ]
Index a tar bundle, or read one file out of one.  With no
.I NAME
this prints how many files the archive holds and then the index: a line per
file, saying its name, where in the archive it begins and how long it is.  With
one, it writes that file to standard output.
.PP
.B \-X bundle
reads a zip and keeps it in memory, which is right for a paper's own support
files and wrong for the other kind of bundle: TeX Live is gigabytes, a document
reads a few dozen files out of it, and nothing should have to hold the rest.  A
tar has no directory \(em finding a file means reading every header up to it
\(em so the index supplies the one thing it lacks, and a file becomes a seek and
a read.  The same index makes an archive fetchable a file at a time with a range
request each, which is how tectonic ships TeX Live without shipping TeX Live.
.PP
Reading is held against
.BR tar (1)
itself: the same names it lists, the same bytes it extracts, for a name too long
for a header field, for files whose lengths straddle the 512-byte block, and for
a file of nothing at all.
.TP
.BI \-X " map \fIFILE\fR.map [" \fINAME\fR ]
Read a font map.  With no
.I NAME
this prints how many fonts it defines, how many are re-encoded and how many are
slanted or extended; with one, what that TeX font name means.
.PP
This is the join between everything else.  A
.I .dvi
names a font
.BR ptmr8r ,
and nothing in the file says what that is: the name is a TeX name, and the map
is what turns it into a real font file, an encoding to read it through, and
whatever slanting or extending the document asked for \(em which is how
.B \etextsl
gets a slanted Times without a slanted Times existing.  Without the map a driver
has a font name and no font.
.PP
A line is a small grammar with no separators: the first word is the TeX name, a
second bare word is the font's PostScript name, a
.B <
names a file whose kind comes from its extension, and a quoted run is PostScript
to run before the font is used.
.TP
.BI \-X " enc \fIFILE\fR.enc"
Read an encoding file: a PostScript array of 256 glyph names, which is what
makes the codes in a
.I .dvi
mean something in a font that has never heard of TeX.  Prints each code the
encoding uses and the glyph it names.
.PP
The map and the encodings are checked against the installation they describe:
every line of the installation's own
.I pdftex.map
must read, every file a line names must be one
.BR kpsewhich (1)
finds, and the whole chain \(em a TeX font name, its map entry, its encoding,
its Type 1 font and the
.I .tfm
TeX set the document with \(em must agree about which glyph a code is and how
wide it is.
.TP
.BI \-X " pfb \fIFILE\fR.pfb [" \fICHAR\fR ]
Read a Type 1 font.  With no
.I CHAR
this prints its name, its matrix and bounding box, how many glyphs and
subroutines it holds and whether it carries its own encoding; with one, which
glyph that character is and how wide.
.PP
This is the font Computer Modern actually ships as, and the one nearly every
.I .dvi
of the last thirty years is drawn with.  It is a PostScript program in two
halves \(em a cleartext header saying what the font is called and how it is
encoded, and an encrypted body holding the outlines.  The encryption is Adobe's
and was never a secret: the keys are published, and the point of them was to
keep the outlines from being edited by hand.  It is applied twice, once to the
body and again to each glyph.
.PP
A glyph's width is inside its charstring rather than beside it \(em the first
thing a Type 1 glyph does is
.BR hsbw ,
which declares its left side bearing and its advance \(em so reading it means
decrypting the charstring and decoding the numbers at the front of it.  The
outlines are kept as they came: a driver embeds a charstring, it does not
interpret one.
.PP
Every glyph of four Computer Modern fonts is compared with
.BR t1disasm (1)
in the test suite, and every width with the
.I .afm
beside the font and the
.I .tfm
TeX sets it with.
.TP
.BI \-X " otf \fIFILE\fR.otf [" \fICHAR\fR ]
Read an OpenType or TrueType font.  With no
.I CHAR
this prints its names, what it is measured in, how many glyphs and characters it
holds, and its table directory; with one, which glyph that character becomes,
what the font calls it, and how wide it is.
.PP
This is the modern half of the font problem.  A
.I .tfm
says how wide a character is and a
.I .pk
says what its pixels are, but a document set in an OpenType font names the file
itself, and everything a driver needs is inside it.  The container is a
directory of tables; the ones read here are the ones a driver reads before it
can place a glyph \(em
.BR head ,
.BR hhea ,
.BR maxp ,
.BR hmtx ,
.BR name ,
.B cmap
and
.BR post .
A font with CFF outlines keeps its glyph names inside the
.B CFF
rather than in
.BR post ,
so that is read too \(em its INDEXes, its dictionaries and its charset \(em
along with the width at the front of each Type 2 charstring, which is the same
number
.B hmtx
states and arrives by an entirely different road.  The outlines themselves are
not interpreted: a driver embeds those.
.PP
A font collection is read as its first font.  Every table, every name, every
character of the
.B cmap
and every glyph name of four fonts is compared with
.BR otfinfo (1)
in the test suite.
.TP
.BI \-X " pk \fIFILE\fR.pk [" \fICHAR\fR ]
Read a packed bitmap font.  With no
.I CHAR
this prints its design size, checksum, resolution and how many characters it
holds; with one, it draws that character.
.PP
A
.I .pk
holds what a
.I .tfm
does not: the pixels.  It is what a driver drew Computer Modern from for twenty
years, and it is still what
.BR xdvipdfmx (1)
falls back to for a font with no outline.  The format is a compression scheme
rather than a layout: a glyph is the lengths of its runs of black and white, and
those lengths are a stream of nybbles whose meaning depends on a per-character
parameter chosen when the font was packed \(em small numbers take one nybble,
middling ones two, large ones a run of zeros saying how many follow, and a row
that repeats says so once.  Getting any of that slightly wrong gives a glyph
that is plausible and not the one in the file, so every pixel of four of the
fonts TeX Live ships is compared with
.BR gftype (1)
in the test suite.
.TP
.BI \-X " vf \fIFILE\fR.vf [" \fICHAR\fR ]
Read a virtual font.  With no
.I CHAR
this prints its design size, its checksum, how many characters it defines and
the real fonts it sets them in; with one, that character's width and the program
that draws it.
.PP
A virtual font has no glyphs.  Each of its characters is a little DVI program
saying what to set in some
.I other
font: in
.BR ptmr7t ,
an
.B A
is one glyph of the real Times, the
.B fi
ligature is an
.B f
and an
.B i
moved together, and a character Times has no glyph for is a rule and a
.B \especial
saying so \(em which is what a driver prints instead of leaving a silent gap.
This is how TeX's own encodings were carried onto fonts laid out differently,
and a
.I .dvi
that names such a font cannot be read without it.
.PP
The packets are DVI, so they are read with the DVI reader rather than a second
copy of it.  Every character of four of the fonts TeX Live ships is compared
with
.BR vftovp (1)
in the test suite.
.TP
.BI \-X " tfm \fIFILE\fR.tfm ["" \fICHAR\fR ]
Read a font's metrics.  With no
.I CHAR
this prints what the font says about itself \(em its family, coding scheme,
design size, checksum, how many characters it defines, and the seven
.B FONTDIMEN
parameters every text font carries.  With one, it prints that character's
width, height, depth and italic correction, and every ligature and kern it takes
part in.
.PP
The argument may be a path or a font name:
.B \-X tfm cmr10
asks
.B kpsewhich
where the installation keeps it.
.PP
A
.I .tfm
is what every TeX engine measures with, and it is entirely a table of indices
\(em a character carries a width index, not a width, because the characters of a
text font share a handful of widths.  Reading one is therefore bounds checking:
a file whose header disagrees with its own tables, or whose index runs past the
table it names, is refused rather than read into whatever lies next to it.
Lengths are printed in units of the design size, as
.BR tftopl (1)
prints them, and the two agree character for character on the Computer Modern
fonts.
.TP
.B \-X bundle list
Print the bundles in the cache with the file each is stored in.
.PP
A
.B preamble
key names files read before the inputs and kept as a FORMAT: a macro file is
compiled once and every later build starts from what it left, which is what
.BR tex (1)
does with
.B \edump
and why LaTeX starts in milliseconds rather than seconds.  A preamble may only
DEFINE \(em category codes, macros,
.B \elet
\(em because a format holds state and has nowhere to keep work.  One that does
something (a
.BR \emessage ,
a
.B \ecount
assignment) is not made into a format and the document is compiled whole
instead, since dropping what it did would change what the document means.
.PP
The inputs are read in order and compiled as one document, and what each of them
hashed to is recorded, so a build says exactly what it read. An output
.B type
is a pipeline this engine has:
.BR messages ,
.B tokens
or
.BR disasm .
There is no
.BR bundle ,
.BR tex_format ,
.B shell_escape
or
.B synctex
key, as there is in tectonic's schema: texrs has no package universe, one
format, no shell and no page, and a key describing machinery that does not exist
would be a promise the engine cannot keep.
.TP
.B \-X new " [" \fIDIR\fR ]
Write a
.I Texrs.toml
and an
.I index.tex
into
.I DIR
(the current directory by default), named after it. The starting document sets
the category codes INITEX leaves ordinary, because one that did not would fail
on its first group. An existing document is never overwritten.
.TP
.B \-X build " [" \-\-profile " \fINAME\fR ]"
Build the document this directory is in, found by walking up, into its
.I build/
directory. With one output the profile need not be named; with several, the one
called
.B default
is used unless
.B \-\-profile
says otherwise.
.TP
.B \-X init
Write a document into the current directory, named after it \(em
.B \-X new .
under the name tectonic gives it, because "make one here" is what a user reaches
for in a directory that already exists.
.TP
.B \-X show
Print what the document is: where its source and build directories are, the
inputs it is made of with the first twelve characters of each one's digest, and
the outputs it can produce with the file each would write. Everything is read
from the document and its inputs, so it says what a build would use rather than
what one used last.
.TP
.B \-X dump " [" \-\-profile " \fINAME\fR ]"
Build a profile to standard output, writing nothing. What it prints is what
.B \-X build
would have written, so a pipeline can read a build without hunting for the file
or leaving one behind.
.TP
.B \-X watch " [" \-\-profile " \fINAME\fR ] [" \-\-interval " \fIMS\fR ]"
Build the document, then build it again whenever an input changes, until
interrupted. Change is decided by the digest of each input rather than by its
mtime, so a file touched but not edited does not rebuild, and neither does an
editor that saves through a temporary file \(em which most do, and which moves
the mtime while the content stands still. A document that does not compile is
reported and the watch carries on: that is the normal state of one being
edited.
.PP
A command that is not one of these is looked for on
.B PATH
as
.BI texrs- name
and run with the remaining arguments, which is how tectonic lets a command be
added without changing the binary:
.B texrs \-X lint
runs
.BR texrs-lint .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS tex's options
Accepted in
.BR tex 's
own spelling, with one dash or two and a value after either an
.B =
or a space, so an invocation written for
.B tex
drives texrs unchanged.  This milestone acts on
.BR \-interaction ;
the rest are recorded and reported by
.B \-X show
but change nothing yet, because what they configure \(em the log file, the
format, the DVI \(em is the stomach texrs does not have.
.TP
.BI \-interaction= MODE
.BR batchmode ", " nonstopmode ", " scrollmode " or " errorstopmode .
.B batchmode
writes nothing to the terminal; texrs never stops to ask, so the other three
differ only in what they would do at an error.
.TP
.BI \-jobname= NAME
Set the job name.
.TP
.BI \-output\-directory= DIR
Write files in
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BI \-progname= NAME
Set the program name.
.TP
.BI \-fmt= NAME
Use a named format, as
.BI & NAME
does.
.TP
.B \-ini
Be initex.  texrs loads no format either way, so it already is.
.TP
.B \-halt\-on\-error
Stop at the first error, which texrs does regardless.
.TP
.BR \-file\-line\-error ", " \-no\-file\-line\-error
Enable or disable
.B file:line:error
style messages.
.TP
.B \-recorder
Record the files read.
.TP
.B \-8bit
Write 8\-bit characters as themselves rather than as
.BR ^^ -escapes.
.SS texrs's options
.TP
.B \-\-repl
Start the interactive prompt.  A line is a line of TeX read with every line
before it still in effect: a
.B \ecatcode
set on one prompt changes how the next one READS, a
.B \edef
changes what it means, and a register assignment survives \(em the session
re-runs the document it has built rather than evaluating each line in a vacuum.
A line that fails is rolled back rather than left in the document, and
.B \eend
ends the line, not the session.  Tab completes the primitives; history lives in
.IR ~/.texrs/history .
With stdin on a pipe the line editor is skipped and lines are read plainly, so
.B texrs \-\-repl < doc.tex
works.
.TP
.B \-\-lsp
Speak the Language Server Protocol over stdio.  Completion and hover come from
the same primitive corpus that generates the reference documentation, and
diagnostics come from the engine's own lowerer \(em a document the editor shows
as clean is a document
.B texrs
will run.  Nothing is written to the terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-dap
Speak the Debug Adapter Protocol over stdio: source\-line breakpoints, single
stepping, a stack frame, and the
.B \ecount
registers as the variables scope.  What is debuggable is what survives
lowering \(em macros expand at COMPILE time, so a breakpoint stops on the lines
that left run\-time work behind, and one set on a line that did not is reported
unverified rather than silently never firing.  The debug run is interpreted:
the tracing JIT would compile the markers away.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-tokens
Print the mouth's token stream for the file and exit.  No expansion happens,
so what prints is what the category codes made of the bytes.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-ast
Print the command stream the frontend lowered the document to, and exit.  TeX
has no expression grammar to parse into a tree \(em the mouth and the expander
hand the stomach a flat run of primitive commands, and that run IS this
frontend's AST.  It is the stage between the other two listings: expansion has
happened, so a macro appears as what it expanded to, and code generation has
not, so a
.B \eifnum
is still a conditional with two branches rather than a jump.  A tail-recursive
macro shows as the loop it lowered to.  Nothing is written to the bytecode
cache, because nothing reached the code generator.
.TP
.B \-\-disasm
Print the lowered fusevm bytecode disassembly and exit.  A
.B \ecount
register is a VM slot, so
.B \eadvance\ecount0 by 5
reads as
.BR "GetSlot / LoadInt / Add / SetSlot" .
.TP
.B \-\-aot
Compile the document to a standalone native executable beside it \(em
.I doc.tex
becomes
.IR doc ,
the way a C file becomes a program.  The chunk is emitted as a relocatable
object through fusevm's ahead\-of\-time compiler and linked against the texrs
runtime, so the result runs with no interpreter dispatch loop and needs no texrs
on the machine.  It prints exactly what an ordinary run prints.
.IP
Requires a C toolchain for the link step.
.B TEXRS_STATICLIB
names the runtime library to link against when it is not beside the binary.
.TP
.B \-\-tiers
Run the document, then report what fusevm's tiers did with its bytecode: how
many ops the chunk holds, whether the block tier accepts and has compiled it,
the largest block\-eligible op range, every loop header and whether the tracing
tier compiled it, and the ops the block tier refuses with their counts.  The
answers come from fusevm's own predicates, not from an assumption about them \(em
enabling the JIT is not the same as being compiled by it.
.TP
.B \-\-build
Compile the document into the bytecode cache and stop, without running it.  What
a later run would have paid for is paid now, which is what a build step is for:
the cache is warm before anyone is waiting on it.
.TP
.BI \-\-jobs= N
Compile
.I N
documents at once when several files are named, one thread each; the default is
one per core. Output is printed in ARGUMENT order however the threads finish,
because a build log that reorders itself between runs is not one anyone can
diff, and the exit status is the worst of the runs. A single document is
compiled by one thread either way \(em this parallelises the batch, not the
engine.
.TP
.B \-\-build
Compile the document into the bytecode cache and stop, without running it.
A build step that does this leaves the run that follows starting from bytecode:
the mouth, the expander and the lowerer are skipped entirely on a hit.  Nothing
the document does at run time happens \(em no
.B \emessage
is written and no
.B \erust
block is compiled \(em because none of that is compilation.
.TP
.B \-\-dvi
Typeset the document to
.I FILE.dvi
beside the source, and say where it went. The text is measured in
.B cmr10
and broken into lines that fit plain TeX's measure, then stacked down plain
TeX's page. What comes out is a real DVI file: \fBdvitype\fR reads it and
\fBdvipdfmx\fR turns it into a PDF.
.IP
It is not \fBtex\fR's typesetting. Lines are broken first-fit -- the first
break that fits -- where \fBtex\fR considers every feasible set of breakpoints
for a whole paragraph at once and minimises the total badness
(\fItex.web\fR \(sc813). There is no hyphenation, no glue stretching or
shrinking, no page breaking by penalties, one font and no maths. A paragraph set
here and the same paragraph set by \fBtex\fR will not agree line for line.
.TP
.B \-\-text
Print what the document SAYS -- its own words, after every macro has been
expanded -- rather than only the
.B \\message
stream. This is not a typeset page: there is no line breaking, no page and no
font, because those are the stomach's and texrs has none. It is the text, in
order, which is what an engine with a mouth and an expander can honestly
produce.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-cache
Compile this run rather than reading the bytecode cache.  The result is the
same either way; this is how a run is timed against a cold one, or how a cache
that is suspected of being wrong is ruled out.
.TP
.B \-\-cache\-stats
Print where the cache is, how many documents it holds and how large it is, then
exit.  With
.B TEXRS_CACHE
set to
.BR 0 ,
.B false
or
.BR no ,
it says the cache is off and names the path it would have used.
.TP
.B \-\-cache\-clear
Delete the cache and exit.  Every document compiles again on its next run.
Deleting a cache is never destructive: it holds only what can be recompiled.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the logo, the live\-stats box and the option grammar, then exit.  Coloured
when stdout is a terminal and plain down a pipe, so the output can be read by a
person or by a program without either getting the other's version.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version banner and exit.
.SH INTERCEPTS
.B \eintercept{before|after|around}{<glob>}{\ehandler}
registers advice on macro expansion:
.RS
.nf
\edef\egreet#1{HELLO-#1}
\edef\etrace{[in]}
\eintercept{before}{greet}{\etrace}
\emessage{\egreet{WORLD}}      % => [in]HELLO-WORLD
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B before
puts the handler's body in front of the expansion,
.B after
puts it behind, and
.B around
replaces it, with
.B \eproceed
standing for what the macro would have expanded to \(em a handler with no
.B \eproceed
suppresses the call.
.PP
The pattern is a GLOB over macro names, which is what makes advice useful on a
macro package: it is registered before the macros it will catch exist.  The
handler is a macro that takes no parameters; one that takes them is refused
rather than called with whatever followed the intercepted call.
.PP
Expansion is a compile\-time act, so advice is woven into the token stream and
is undone by the group that registered it, like any other assignment.  A handler
that calls the macro it advises does not weave itself: a call inside advice is
not advised.
.SH INLINE RUST
A
.B \erust{ \&... }
block is compiled by
.BR rustc ,
loaded, and its
.B #[no_mangle] pub extern "C"
functions become callable from the document:
.RS
.nf
\erust{
    #[no_mangle]
    pub extern "C" fn twice(n: i64) -> i64 { n * 2 }
}
\ecatcode`\e{=1 \ecatcode`\e}=2
\ecount1=21
\emessage{\erustcall twice \ecount1 \eendrust}   % => 42
.fi
.RE
.PP
The block body is Rust, not TeX, so it is lifted out of the file BEFORE the
mouth reads it \(em
.BR # ,
.BR { ,
.B }
and
.B &
are category codes the mouth would act on.  A call is a NUMBER wherever TeX
reads one: a register assignment, an arithmetic operand, a conditional, or a
message body.  In running text it is called for its effect and the value is
dropped.
.PP
The compiled library is cached by body hash, so only the first run pays for the
compile.  A block that does not compile stops the run with rustc's own
diagnostic;
.B RUSTC
names the compiler to use.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
The file could not be read, an option was not recognised, or the engine
stopped.  Engine errors are written to stderr as
.BR "! <reason>." ,
the way tex writes them.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Run a file and see its
.B \emessage
stream, printed the way tex prints it:
.RS
.nf
texrs hello.tex
(./hello.tex HELLO\-WORLD )
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read the bytecode a document lowered to:
.RS
.nf
texrs \-\-disasm examples/counters.tex
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read the token stream the mouth produced:
.RS
.nf
texrs \-\-dump\-tokens examples/macros.tex
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.cache/texrs/scripts.rkyv
The bytecode cache: an rkyv shard of source path to compiled
.B fusevm::Chunk
, written after a document compiles and read instead of compiling it again.
Entries carry the source's mtime and the mtime of the texrs that wrote them, so
an edited document or a rebuilt texrs is a miss rather than a stale hit, and a
shard this build cannot read is rebuilt rather than misread.  On macOS the path
is under
.IR ~/Library/Caches .
The shard's header carries the magic
.B TEXR
and follows the header convention the sibling engines share, so
.BR zdbview (1)
opens it as a key/value cache.
.TP
.I examples/*.tex
Runnable examples, each held in parity with real tex by the test suite.
.TP
.I tests/cases/*.tex
The differential corpus.
.TP
.I tests/known_gaps.txt
Every case that does not match tex yet, with the reason.
